<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="notes-week-09">
    <title>Weeks 9 and 10</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            <alert>Week 9 Reading covers 1.4.2, 1.6, 8.2, and 7.1 through 7.5</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Week 10 reading covers 7.6 through 7.10 and 8.3</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 13 covers 1.4.2, 1.6 (components of cross products), section 8.2 (up to example 8.3), and sections 7.1 and 7.2. </alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 14 Covers 7.3 through 7.5.</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            Note: part of the goal for this week is to finish chapter 7 in class. Sections 7.6 and 7.8,
            as well as note 7.2, will be helpful for the homework.
        </p>
    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-torque">
        <title>Chapters 7 and 8: Week 9</title>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 13 covers 1.4.2, 1.6 (components of cross products), section 8.2 (up to example 8.3), and sections 7.1 and 7.2. </alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <alert>Reading Quiz 14 Covers 7.3 through 7.5.</alert>
        </p>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Start by going over the definition of the cross product. Besides knowing
                        the definition, decide how you want to learn the right hand rule to find
                        the direction of the cross-product. Pick a method and stick with it.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        K&amp;K delay the definition of the angular velocity vector until chapter 8,
                        but knowing the definition know let's us tackle some conceptual subtleties
                        head-on. First, we can't really describe a rotational position as vectors,
                        but angular velocities are regular vectors, and finally we have a vector
                        relation ship between velocity and angular velocity <m>~{\vec{v}=\vec{\omega}\times\vec{r}}</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 7.2 defines the angular momentum of a point particle, introducing
                        a geometrical interpretation and makes two important points: the angular momentum
                        depends on a choice of origin, and it is not, in general, parallel to the
                        angular velocity. We'll have to learn to live with that.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                       Section 7.3 starts setting up the math for what we'll study this week: fixed-axis rotation.
                       The moment of inertia, the rotational analog of mass, makes its first appearance. Note, however,
                       that the moment of inertia depends on the axis of rotation, so it's a fairly different quantity.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 7.4 and 7.5 define the concept of torque and show the relationship
                        <m>\vec{\tau}=\dfrac{d\vec{L}}{dt}</m> for a particle. K&amp;K set up the discussion
                        so you'd expect that this relationship also applies for a rigid body, but the
                        proof doesn't come until section 7.8.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Sections 7.6 and 7.7 are applications of <m>\vec{\tau}=\dfrac{d\vec{L}}{dt}</m> to
                        a variety of problems. Examples 7.10 and 7.12 are fairly typical types of problems.
                        Note that K&amp;K don't really explain why the tension in the string is no longer constant
                        in example 7.10. Can you figure out a physical reason?
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 7.8 finally establishes <m>\vec{\tau}=\dfrac{d\vec{L}}{dt}</m> for a rigid body, by
                        using an important decomposition of the angular momentum of the rigid body,
                        equation 7.16. K&amp;K don't emphasize this point too much, but it is important to note that this
                        relationship holds not only when you take your origin to be a fixed point in the lab, but also
                        when you take the origin to be the center of mass, even when it is accelerated! This is particularly
                        helpful when solving rotational statics problems for an accelerated object: the regular algorithm
                        works provided you take the origin of torques to be the CM.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Section 7.9 shows some of the weaknesses of the energy treatment of 
                        K&amp;K: since the work-energy theorem only deals with the concept
                        of <q>center-of-mass work</q>, a new work-energy theorem has to
                        be invented for <q>rotational work</q>, and example 7.16 is quite clunky. 
                        Example 7.17 shows this clearly. The total kinetic energy has two terms,
                        and mechanical energy is conserved. Similarly for example 7.16: since
                        the drum rolls without slipping, the friction force does no work, and
                        conservation of energy applies.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        That said, knowing that the work <m>\vec{F}\cdot d\vec{r}</m> reduces
                        to <m>\tau d\theta</m> when the point of application of a force is undergoing
                        pure rotation is useful. The constraint that <m>\tau d\theta=d(\frac{1}{2}I\omega^2)</m>
                        can be sometimes easier to apply than the full torque-angular momentum relationship,
                        but it's just a convenience.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        You probably want to spend some time with note 7.2 since this chapter has a lot 
                        of content.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="subsec-week10">
        <title>Chapters 7 and 8: Week 10</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The previous week was theoretically very dense, and we mostly spent it
                        defining new concepts: torque, angular momentum, and moment of inertia.
                        We also established useful relationships between these for what is called
                        <term>planar motion</term> or <term>fixed-axis rotation</term>. A good place
                        to start this week is Note 7.2 to make sure you understand the definitions
                        and what the relationships mean.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        One thing to keep in mind is that, although I will often use terms like
                        <q>rotational kinetic energy</q> or <q>angular momentum of the center of
                        mass</q>, which references equations (7.23) and (7.18), keep in mind
                        that ultimately, the kinetic energy of a body is just the sum of the kinetic
                        energies of the individual particles, and the angular momentum is just the
                        sum of the angular momenta of the individual particles.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        That said, (7.4), (7.23), and (7.18) are very useful, so you should understand 
                        what they are telling you. When you can describe the motion of a rigid body as
                        a fixed axis rotation, then you can say that <m>~{K=\frac{1}{2}I\omega^2}</m> and
                        <m>~{L_z=I\omega}</m>, with a suitably defined moment of inertia, which may
                        require using the parallel axis theorem.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        If the motion of the CM is not a pure rotation (simplest example: ball rolling
                        down a hill), then (7.18) tells you what to differentiate with respect to time
                        when you use <m>\vec{\tau}=\dfrac{d\vec{L}}{dt}</m>. Similarly, (7.23)
                        tells you what to use when applying the work-energy principle.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        With the theory out of the way, we will spend time solving planar motion problems,
                        as well as rotational statics problems. Really practical stuff to help you see
                        how the concepts come together. Statics in particular is interesting because
                        we can use the results of homework problem 7.1: in static equilibrium, the net
                        torque is independent of the origin, and this freedom allows you to be creative
                        in problem solving.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We will also see some examples of rotational statics for an accelerating object.
                        Here, the results of section 7.8 are crucial: even though the CM is accelerated,
                        you can still set the net torque to zero, provided you use the CM as the origin.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        We will end the week with an example of non-fixed axis rotation, the regular
                        precession of the gyroscope, discussed in section 8.3. This is a type of motion
                        for which your intuition may fail you, as identifying all the forces involved
                        can be quite difficult. That said, the mathematical description is rock solid,
                        and it will be our first step towards growing <em>new intuition</em>, which
                        will serve you well when you study the general motion of a rigid body in 
                        Phys 4011.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </subsection>
</section>